    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and verified end-to-end. Let me summarize what I did.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a structured address form to the owner create/update contract while keeping the flat `address` input backward-compatible. All tests pass (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n### Changes\n\n**1. OpenAPI spec** (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)\n- Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` (both optional, `maxLength: 255`) to the `OwnerFields` schema. Since the `Owner` response schema is `allOf: OwnerFields`, these appear on both the request DTO and the response DTO.\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list so a structured-only request (which sends no flat `address`) passes Bean Validation. `firstName`, `lastName`, `city`, `telephone` remain required. The \"address in either form\" rule is now enforced in the controller.\n- Rewrote the `address` description to document that it's the composed, normalized value when structured fields are present.\n\n**2. `Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` fields (columns `address_line1`/`address_line2`) with getters/setters. MapStruct maps them by name automatically (verified in the generated `OwnerMapperImpl`).\n\n**3. DB schemas** \u2014 added `address_line1`/`address_line2` columns to all four dialects (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n**4. Controller** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`)\n- `normalizeAddress`: prefers structured fields \u2014 when `addressLine1` is non-blank, it normalizes `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` and composes `address` as `normalizedLine1` + (`\" \" + normalizedLine2` when line2 present); otherwise it falls back to normalizing the flat `address` and clears the structured fields.\n- Validation is unchanged in shape: because the composed `address` is non-blank iff either form was supplied, the existing required-field check (`requireText(\"address\", \u2026)`) now correctly accepts an owner supplying a non-blank `addressLine1` OR a flat `address`, plus city.\n- `updateOwner` now carries the structured fields through.\n\nSince the household hash, postcode validation, and locality derive from `postcode`/`city` (not the address string), everything that reads the stored `address` now sees the structured-composed value when present, satisfying the fallback requirement.\n\nThe core test confirms `addressLine1: \"10 king st\"` is stored/returned as `\"10 KING STREET\"` and `address` equals the same composed value, and the flat-address path still returns 2xx.",
